===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|en|tallyho}}
{{clipping|en|tallyho}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtæli/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-tally.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-tally.ogg|(US)}}
* {{hyphenation|en|tal|ly}}
* {{rhymes|en|æli|s=2}}
* {{hmp|en|telly<aa:celery-salary merger>}}

====Interjection====
{{en-intj}}

# {{lb|en|radio|aviation}} Target sighted.
#: ''(Air Traffic Control): Speedbird 123, New York, traffic at two o’clock, seven miles, a Boeing 737, west-bound, at 4000 feet.''
#: ''(Pilot): New York, Speedbird 123, '''tally'''.''

=====Usage notes=====
In aviation radio usage, more common than original {{m|en|tallyho}}. In civilian aviation usage, the official term for “traffic sighted” is “traffic in sight”.<ref>Federal Aviation Administration: [https://web.archive.org/web/20141013230622/http://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/PCG/Index.htm Pilot/Controller Glossary (P/CG)], [https://web.archive.org/web/20140912024001/http://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/ATPubs/PCG/T.HTM T] (Traffic)</ref>

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|target sighted}} {{l|en|tallyho}}

===Etymology 2===
{{etymid|en|count}}
From {{inh|en|enm|talie}}, from {{der|en|xno|tallie}} and {{der|en|fro|taille|t=notch in a piece of wood signifying a debt}}, from {{der|en|la-med|tallia}}, from {{der|en|la|talea|t=a cutting, rod, stick}}. {{doublet|en|taille|talea}}.

The same root word also appears in {{cog|sv|talja}}, {{cog|da|talje||tally}}, which are related to {{cog|non-oen|telgja|tælgia|to carve}}, which may be related to the English term (compare {{m|en|score}}, to {{ncog|non|skor||notch}}). Also compare {{ncog|gem-pro|*talą||number}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtæli/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-tally.ogg|(US)}}
* {{hyphenation|en|tal|ly}}
* {{rhymes|en|æli|s=2}}
* {{hmp|en|telly<aa:celery-salary merger>}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{abbreviation of|en|tally stick}}.
# {{lb|en|by extension}} One of two books, sheets of paper, etc., on which corresponding [[account]]s were kept.
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Any account or score kept by notches or marks, whether on wood or paper, or in a book, especially one kept in [[duplicate]].
#* {{quote-web
|en
|date=September 2, 2011
|author=Phil McNulty
|title=Bulgaria 0-3 England
|work=BBC
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/14770875.stm
|page=
|passage=Bulgaria, inevitably, raised the tempo in the opening moments of the second half and keeper Joe Hart was forced into his first meaningful action to block a deflected corner - but England were soon threatening to add to their goal '''tally'''.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120131003831/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/14770875.stm|archivedate=31 January 2012}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} One thing made to [[suit]] another; a [[counterpart]] or [[match]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1690|author=w:John Dryden|title=Don Sebastian|section=Act V, scene 1
|passage=So paired, so suited in their minds and persons,<br>That they were framed the '''tallies''' for each other.}}
# A notch, mark, or score made on or in a tally; as, to make or earn a score or tally in a game.
# A [[tally shop]].
# A ribbon on a sailor's cap bearing the name of the ship or the (part of) the navy to which they belong.
# {{lb|en|informal|regional|dated}} A state of [[cohabitation]], living with another individual in an [[intimate]] relationship outside of [[marriage]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1884|author=Ben Bierley|title=Tales and Sketches of Lancashire Life|page=47|text=But I’d advise thee t’ live '''tally''' for o that, if thou con mak it reet wi’ some owd damsel, ut does no’ care what folk say’n about owt o’th’sort.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=OHvTzFDR3h8C&pg=PA47 |chapter=Treadlepin Fold}}
# {{lb|en|UK|obsolete}} Five dozen [[bunch]]es of [[turnip]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1851|author={{w|Henry Mayhew}}|title={{w|London Labour and the London Poor}}
|passage=Then turnips isn't such good sale yet, but they may be soon, for winter's best for them. {{...}} I buy turnips by the ''''tally'''.' A tally's five dozen bunches.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1890|journal=Gardeners' Chronicle|volume=7|page=15
|passage=A bunch of Turnips, 12 and upwards. A bunch of Leeks, 6 and upwards. Bunches of Greens and of herbs vary much, according to kind, size, and season. Roll of Celery = 6, 8, to 12 heads. A '''tally''' = five dozen.}}

